WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.670
Five years ago, I
decided to learn more

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:13.420
about two exciting frameworks
that I kept hearing about more

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.990
and more often everywhere,
really, in bars, restaurants.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.150
It got really spooky.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.620
The first is the R tidyverse
from Hadley's and Garrett's

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.540
great book, R for Data Science.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.750
And some of you might
have heard of it, maybe.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.720
And the second one is the
Kaggle machine learning and data

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.400
science platform.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.530
And little did I
know at the time

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.200
that the combination
of those frameworks

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.870
would transform my
career and open up

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.780
a whole new world of
exciting challenges.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.380
And much like for our
dragonball friends there.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.560
This fusion really
boosted my power level.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.070
So today I would
like to show you

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:58.030
how the community can help
you to grow, to learn,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.890
and to inspire others.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.140
And whether you're
a regular on Kaggle,

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.140
you've only ever dipped your
toes into the community,

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.950
or you've never
heard of it at all,

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.880
I hope that my talk
and my story will

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.630
inspire you to make the most
out of the amazing opportunities

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.330
that the Kaggle
community provides,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.540
and that in turn, you will
then share your great ideas

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.920
and creativity with
the rest of us.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.710
"Now that sounds
great," you might say.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.590
"I have a very small
question, though.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.200
What is Kaggle?"

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.290
Well, I'm glad you asked.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.900
Kaggle.com was
launched 13 years ago

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.690
as a website for machine
learning competitions.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.420
And that means that
the goal is to build

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.570
competitive predictive models
on a specific machine learning

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.820
problem with the best
models in the end,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.430
scoring highest on an
objective leaderboard.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.460
And while competitions remain
one of the main cornerstones

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.130
today, Kaggle covers
many key aspects

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.410
of the data science landscape.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.070
Users can upload and
curate their own data sets.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.100
Lively discussion forums
host many conversations

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.199
about machine learning
and data science,

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:12.430
and kagglers can build, run,
and publish their own notebooks

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.710
directly on the site.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:18.340
And there's so much to learn
here, so much to discover.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.890
But I also realize that all
these possibilities can be

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.860
a little bit confusing, right?

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.830
Maybe intimidating when you're
joining the site at first,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.030
and I get it.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.240
I really do.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.420
When I joined Kaggle back in
the day, I felt the same.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.820
I felt a little
bit hesitant when

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.220
I saw all these smart people
and their clever ideas.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.580
So much so, in
fact, that I decided

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:44.800
to join up anonymously under
the pseudonym heads or tails.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.060
Nobody knew who I really was.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.300
And that helped alleviate
much of the pressure

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.980
that I would have
otherwise put myself

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.120
under when it comes to actively
participating in the community.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.160
So with this little
trick, I felt

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.860
free to work my regular
job during the day

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.970
and be on Kaggle in my secret
identity in the evenings

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.760
and well, nights.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.530
But what I quickly realized is
that much like the R community,

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.780
the Kaggle community
was exceptionally

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.670
welcoming and supportive
of its new members.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.350
And it remains so to this day.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.440
The other trick that helped
me was at first to only

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.990
focus on one aspect of
Kaggle rather than trying

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:27.430
to do everything at once.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:31.690
And in my case, I gravitated
towards the notebooks.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.300
You see, I'm a
very visual person,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.640
and my academic
background had trained

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.950
me to write coherent reports.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:41.410
And my goal on
Kaggle was to learn

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:42.830
new tools and new methods.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:46.000
And I always learn best when
I can apply these new tools

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.130
on a practical problem.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:51.820
So it came natural to me to
start writing and publishing

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.855
notebooks to learn and to share
what I learned with others.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.480
And so today my talk will be
revolving around the notebooks.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.980
But much of what
I'm telling you can

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.440
be translated to
other aspects as well,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.470
like discussions or
competitions, or also

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.930
courses which might be
more relevant for you

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.840
and your goals.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.120
And all these aspects
are representative

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.209
of Kaggle as a whole,
and in turn they

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.020
also contain smaller areas that
are representative of them.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:27.340
So in a sense, much
like a cauliflower,

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.030
this is a fractal approach
and a fractal talk

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.100
in which I would
like to show you

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.970
that I can be less than just
a one dimensional speaker.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.630
I'd take that.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.910
So I started learning
the tidyverse

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:46.120
and to write notebooks, to
practice what I had learned.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.550
And I also started
reading the notebooks

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.560
that other people
were putting out.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.950
And that really
exploded my horizons.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.940
There was so much creativity,
so many great ideas out there.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.570
To give you an example.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.460
I remember when I
first discovered

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:05.320
this notebook written by
Jonathan in R on US flight

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.580
tracking data.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.240
And I had never worked with
geospatial data before.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.480
And his map visuals, they
just blew my mind. The way

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.420
that he presented his
insights was so clean

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.080
and well thought out.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.600
There's a lot going
on in this map

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.475
that the different
cities, the flight routes,

00:05:21.475 --> 00:05:23.800
but the design choices
make this visual

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.660
very clear and accessible.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.370
So I wanted to learn
about geospatial data.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.370
And then this
competition launched,

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.850
which was about predicting
taxi trip durations in New York

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:37.180
city, but it was really
about writing notebooks

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.990
that other people would find
useful, exploring the data,

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:44.440
breaking it down, finding
all the hidden insights.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.750
So I took my
exploratory approach

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.400
and I started to
learn from scratch

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.100
about maps and geocoordinates
and all these things.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.000
And this is my very
first interactive leaflet

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.910
map that I built for this
notebook that I wrote,

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.730
and I'm still
using leaflet today

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:02.800
based on what I learned
from this experience.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.380
It's a great tool.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.690
And even better, my
notebook ended up

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.240
receiving the most community
votes in this competition

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.710
and winning me a prize.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.160
I was awesome.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.490
I mean, I was over the moon.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:15.250
[applause] Oh, Thank you.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.520
Thank you.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.840
Yeah and I had found
my new passion.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.850
So over the following
months, I jumped

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:25.800
into almost every new
competition that was launched.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.850
So that I could learn
about the specific tools

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:32.570
that were needed to
understand the data.

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:37.430
And my goal was to
be the first person

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.280
to write a comprehensive
exploratory data analysis

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.390
for the competition data.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.080
So that when new people
joined the competition,

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.060
they could get a jumpstart.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.310
So EDA became my
focus and it allowed

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:52.520
me to discover and to
share lots of cool stuff.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.090
So for instance, I joined a time
series forecasting competition

00:06:56.090 --> 00:07:00.290
to learn about methods like
ARIMA or tools like Prophet.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:04.130
And this particular example
was about predicting web views

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.370
for specific international
Wikipedia pages.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:08.900
So here in this
example plot, you

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:12.800
see that the views for the wiki
page of the band Twenty One Pilots

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.518
exploded dramatically
in early 2016.

00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:16.560
I don't really know them.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.852
I don't know why that happened.

00:07:17.852 --> 00:07:22.310
But there are some
interesting lag effects here

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.640
as to how this rise happened
in the different countries that

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.550
are coded by different colors
and in some interesting lags

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:30.710
in the spikes too, that pop up.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:33.290
So features like this
made this competition

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:36.040
interesting and challenging.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.130
And I also joined my
first NLP competitions,

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:42.400
learning how to use Julia's
and David's great tidytext

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:43.160
package.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:47.170
And in this particular one I was
flexing my storytelling muscles

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:49.570
and I wrote my own
chilling short story

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.320
around the analysis of language
used by classic horror writers

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.570
like Shelley or Lovecraft.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.340
Right? so you see
that love and death

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.070
are there at the center
of most of these stories,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:03.820
intertwined in an
eternal battle.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:06.400
And with every new notebook
and every new competition,

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.640
I was learning something new.
New tools, new tricks,

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.610
and new ideas from reading
other people's notebooks

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.450
and exchanging
thoughts with them.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.780
You see, one of the great
things about Kaggle notebooks

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.820
is that you can
fork and tweak them

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.720
directly on the website using
this little button up there.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.480
And you see this often with
competition modeling code,

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.790
and people build on
each other's models

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.700
and make them
better step by step.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.730
The Kaggle community is
very good at learning

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:35.530
from each other,
and it's standing

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.380
on each other's shoulders to
see further than we otherwise

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:39.880
could.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:41.679
And myself,

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:44.650
I owe a lot to the support
of the Kaggle community,

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:48.760
which made me the very first
notebook's grandmaster, which

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.400
is a title that you can
earn through community

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.377
votes on your
notebooks, and then

00:08:53.377 --> 00:08:55.210
you can take your
achievements and your work

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:57.490
and you can showcase
it to the world

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:01.570
through your Kaggle profile,
for instance, to recruiters.

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.970
So this is my profile,
which kind of summarizes

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.710
my top notebooks
and data sets and so

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.600
on in a very comprehensive way.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.390
And I'm pretty positive
that it helped me

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:14.920
a lot in my transition from
the ivory towers of academia

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.950
to doing data science
in the real world.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.930
And a lot of the
interviews that I

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:22.030
had with various
companies, the lessons

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:26.513
that I had learned on
Kaggle, they were invaluable.

00:09:26.513 --> 00:09:27.930
And I'm sure the
whole grandmaster

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.080
thing didn't hurt either.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.080
Right, it's like.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:31.870
But, Yeah.

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:35.130
So I believe that the use
of Kaggle as a portfolio

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.750
remains very
underrated even today.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.450
It's a great way to
showcase your work,

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:40.750
especially in notebooks.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.840
And I would really like
to encourage you to make

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.390
your Kaggle notebooks public.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.940
I've read a lot of notebooks
over the last five years,

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.430
and virtually all of them
had something interesting

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.160
that I could learn, and
I felt very grateful

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:54.870
towards the authors.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:59.910
In fact, two years ago, I
decided to start an initiative

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:01.980
around the Kaggle notebooks.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.130
So let me tell you
about my initiative.

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:10.120
Oh, no, no, not that one.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:11.680
No, no, no.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.610
That's not the one either.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:14.660
Oh, man.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:17.270
I'm dating myself with
that reference, right?

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.100
It's been a while.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.980
No, what I'm talking
about is an initiative

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:26.240
to discover and highlight
great Kaggle notebooks, which,

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.010
for whatever reason,
haven't gotten

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:32.240
the attention and kudos from
the community that they deserve.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.710
I'm calling them hidden gems,
and after reading hundreds

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.020
of notebooks and writing
quite a few myself,

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:43.220
I would like to share with you
what Kaggle taught me on how

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.100
to write a great notebook.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.980
So here are a few tips
that will be illustrated

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.490
by different hidden gems.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:55.340
So first of all, you want
to introduce your work

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:57.500
to capture your
reader's attention

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.900
and draw them into
your analysis.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.540
A couple of topical
images or illustrations

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.810
go always well with a
few opening paragraphs

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:07.940
that explain the
goals, the context,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:10.040
and set the scene
for what's to follow.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.610
So like Laura here, you could
use an image of cocoa beans

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:16.730
to lead into your flavorful
analysis of chocolate taste

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.350
ratings.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:22.350
Explaining your methodology
will help your reader

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:25.290
to follow your analysis
through its various steps.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.060
And this can be part
of your introduction,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.270
and you can even go
as far as Ramshankar did

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.330
and build a flowchart out
of little pictograms that

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:36.100
explain the different
steps of your analysis.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:39.090
And this is really some next
level methodology communication

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.030
right there.

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:43.220
Great visuals,

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.960
of course, they are

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.710
they are at the heart of
many fantastic notebooks.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:51.810
And whether it's for exploration
or for communication,

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.270
you want your data
visualizations

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:58.590
to be clear and accessible
in communicating the insights

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.360
that you derived.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.640
And that can mean
simple visuals,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.390
but it can also mean
complex but well designed.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:11.400
So in this example here,
Daniel was creating a visual

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:15.300
for climate models and rising
CO2 levels that would not

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:17.580
look out of place in
a science or nature

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:19.900
paper on some high
profile website.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:21.660
Right? it's
meticulously designed

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.270
with insets and
annotations and definitely

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:25.840
takes a little time to digest.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.378
So like with all of them,
if you want to learn more,

00:12:28.378 --> 00:12:29.795
definitely check
out the notebook.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.830
But visuals themselves
are not enough, right?

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.860
You also want to
document your findings

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:39.770
and what you read
in those visuals,

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:42.172
not just for your audience,
but also for yourself.

00:12:42.172 --> 00:12:43.880
So that if you come
back to your notebook

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.640
maybe six months later,
you will thank past you

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.640
for writing down what was so
interesting about these plots

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.180
that you made.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:55.010
Right, and Parul here
demonstrates this very well

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:57.530
with her points
to note, complete

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:01.010
with a little pictogram
as a visual anchor.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.050
And documentation is, of
course, the cousin of narration.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.110
So you always want to
have a nice narrative

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:09.260
flow that leads your reader
through your analysis from one

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.450
step to another.

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:14.450
As my final tip,
for today, at least,

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:17.720
you can take your
visualizations and exploration

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:23.700
from the exploration of data
to the explanation of models.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.370
And one of the most
outstanding hidden gems,

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:30.180
Jack here was charting
the journey of an image

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.770
through a neural network.

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.210
Which is a fantastic
title, by the way.

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:37.890
Titles are important, too,
but what I wanted to focus on

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.860
is the way in which he uses
the data visualizations

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:44.460
to show how this handwritten
digit gets transformed

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:47.010
through the different
layers of the neural network

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.240
all the way down to the
classification layer.

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:51.260
And what happens in between.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:51.760
All right.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:53.760
So if you want to learn
more about deep learning

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.850
or if you just want to
read something really cool,

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.150
check out Jack's notebook.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.095
It's great.

00:14:00.095 --> 00:14:01.470
So some of you
might have noticed

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.710
that all of these examples that
I showed you for hidden gems

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.150
were from Python notebooks.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:09.100
As a bit of a contrast
to the earlier R

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:10.460
notebooks that I showed.

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:13.780
And today, Python is definitely
the most popular language

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:17.150
on Kaggle, especially for
deep learning applications.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.360
But there's still plenty
of great R content

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.180
around. In 300 hidden gems
that are collected so far,

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:26.470
I found that the ratio was 3/4
of the notebooks were in Python

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:29.870
and 1/4 in R, and
it shouldn't stop you

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.450
if a notebook is
in Python, right,

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:33.610
you can still learn
a lot from it.

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.770
In fact, one neat
way of learning

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:38.350
might be to take the ideas
from a Python notebook

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:42.040
and translate them into
R, right, or vice versa.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:45.610
That might be a good way for
you to get started on Kaggle,

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:48.310
but for the closing
thought of this talk,

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:50.320
I might have an even
better way for you

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:52.920
to start your own
Kaggle journey.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:56.400
Because I've put
together a Kaggle dataset

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:59.490
